## External login trusts the email claim without checking email_verified

### Status
Fixed.

### GitHub Issue
None yet.

### Summary
`ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs` marks a new account's email confirmed based only on whether the
submitted form value matches the provider's `Email` claim — it never checks the standard OIDC
`email_verified` claim, which is what Google actually provides to answer that exact question.

### Reported Behavior
Not user-reported — found during review of #94. `Program.cs`'s `AddGoogle` registration doesn't
map or request the `email_verified` claim at all, so it isn't available on `info.Principal`, and
`OnPostConfirmationAsync`'s `emailIsProviderVerified` check relies purely on a string comparison
between the `Email` claim and `Input.Email`.

### Expected Behavior
`EmailConfirmed` is only set `true` for a first-time Google sign-in when Google's own
`email_verified` claim confirms it — not merely because the claim value happens to equal what
the user typed into the editable confirmation-form field.

### Root Cause
Matching `Email` claim to `Input.Email` proves the user didn't retype a different address on the
form, but it does not prove the address itself is verified — that's a separate fact Google
reports via `email_verified`, which this code never requests or reads.

### Proposed Fix
1. In `Program.cs`'s `AddGoogle` options, add
   `options.ClaimActions.MapJsonKey("email_verified", "email_verified", ClaimValueTypes.Boolean);`
   so the claim is actually surfaced on `info.Principal`.
2. In `ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`'s `OnPostConfirmationAsync`, require
   `info.Principal.FindFirst("email_verified")?.Value == "true"` in addition to the existing
   `Email`-claim-equals-`Input.Email` check before setting `EmailConfirmed = true`.

Not building a general multi-provider verification-policy abstraction for this now — Google is
the only external provider in use today.

### Acceptance Criteria
- `info.Principal` carries an `email_verified` claim after a Google sign-in.
- A first-time Google sign-in whose `Input.Email` matches the provider's `Email` claim, and whose
  `email_verified` claim is `true`, gets `EmailConfirmed = true` and is signed in directly.
- A first-time Google sign-in whose `email_verified` claim is `false` or missing gets a
  confirmation email and lands on the confirmation-required page, even when `Input.Email`
  matches the provider's `Email` claim exactly.

### Out of Scope
- Any other external provider — not in use today; a shared/reusable verification-policy helper
  is deferred until a second provider is actually added.
